I like the surprise too.  You wouldn't have to look at the public
development site if you wanted to be surprised.  A separate nethack ng
or some web forum would evolve for discussion of the next version's
development.  And given the ambient level of koppiness, it's likely
"inappropriate" discussion would be shouted away here.

 
> > Too much duplicated and dead-end effort goes on with
> > all the patches, and the overall development process is stagnant - I
> > don't care what marvels are finally revealed in the next release, 3
> > years between releases, and hundreds of "resolved" bugs (some of them
> > critical to gameplay) rotting in a queue on a web page, are ridiculous
> > for such a popular game with such an active community.  There are many
> > talented programmers and players with excellent ideas and time to
> > implement them, which could be bringing us fun new releases more
> > efficiently and more quickly.
> 
> Well, nobody keeps those talented programmers to release their own
> versions of NetHack. This is how SlashEm started.


Why ever coordinate on any software project - or on anything - then?
Why try to combine the talents of interested people?   Why not always
have multiple groups or islands of people working in parallel,
duplicating effort?  Hell, why bother writing software libraries?

There's a couple nethack spinoff projects that have gained some good
traction, but I think most of the work out there is born out of lack
of ability to truly contribute, rather than a desire to strike out on
one's own.  And frequently, an idea for a feature will be discussed,
but no one wants to do it because of the hugeness of the project -
think portable bones or OO nethack.  With central, coordinated
development and division of labor, these things could have happened
years ago.  The current devtem paradigm stands in the way.  It matters
that people believe their work is "official".

 
